    "title": "How Does Hofstra University Rank Among America's Best Colleges?",
    "text": "Hofstra University is a private institution that was founded in 1935. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Hofstra University is ranked No. #183 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #99 in Best Value Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 6,581 (fall 2024), its setting is suburban, and the campus size is 244 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Hofstra University is 13:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Hofstra University accepts the Common Application and has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's tuition and fees are $59,960. Sixty-five percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $34,152. The four-year graduation rate is 61%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $52,131.Hofstra University is a private institution that was founded in 1935. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Hofstra University is ranked No. #183 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #99 in Best Value Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 6,581 (fall 2024), its setting is suburban, and the campus size is 244 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Hofstra University is 13:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Hofstra University accepts the Common Application and has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's tuition and fees are $59,960. Sixty-five percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $34,152. The four-year graduation rate is 61%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $52,131.",
